// Investment Readiness Assessment: chapters 6 (capacity-building needs) and 7 (enabling environment)
module.exports = [
  { h: 1, t: "Capacity-Building Needs" },
  { p: "This chapter identifies what MSMEs, BSOs and financial institutions need in order to improve investment readiness and make financing more inclusive. The needs follow from the business models, financial practices and readiness ratings in Chapters 3 to 5, and from the enabling-environment evidence in Chapter 7." },
  { h: 2, t: "MSMEs" },
  { b: [
    "**Supplier readiness:** meeting anchor buyers' quality, volume, reliability and price standards; delivery scheduling; packaging and food safety for produce suppliers.",
    "**Product development:** moving crafts from generic curios to market-led, storytelling-driven design (MD p. 68); developing décor and furnishing lines to lodge specifications.",
    "**Aggregation and collective action:** forming and governing cooperatives or producer groups to achieve volume and bargaining power (MD p. 75).",
    "**Financial management:** separating business and household money, simple bookkeeping, costing and pricing, cash-flow planning across the season, using mobile money to build a trail.",
    "**Formalisation and compliance:** registration, tax clearance and VAT, insurance, licences and certification, sequenced to buyer and lender requirements. Given the cost and time of formalising (Section 7.1), MSMEs also need practical help through the procedures, not only information about them.",
    "**Contracting:** understanding and negotiating supply agreements, payment terms and delivery obligations, which matters more where courts are slow to enforce contracts (Section 7.1).",
    "**Marketing and visibility:** digital listing, trade shows and operator familiarisation (MD p. 75).",
    "**Technical and service skills:** guiding quality (MD p. 84), hospitality, vehicle and boat maintenance, recycling technology.",
    "**Investment readiness:** preparing a business plan and loan application, understanding product terms, negotiating with lenders.",
  ] },
  { p: "The priority gaps differ by chain. The main gaps in each priority chain are linked below to the interventions recommended in Chapter 10 and to the partners best placed to deliver them." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "MSME capacity gaps and recommended interventions by value chain",
    cols: [1500, 2600, 2726, 2200],
    head: ["Value chain", "Priority capacity gaps", "Recommended interventions", "Lead delivery partners (indicative)"],
    rows: [
      ["Food and agricultural supply", "Aggregation; consistent quality and volume; food safety and packaging; VAT and tax clearance; cash flow between delivery and payment", "Producer-group formation; irrigation and post-harvest support; lodge supply agreements; contract-backed working capital", "Anchor lodges, LEA, SMEDCO, CEEC, agricultural extension, conservancies"],
      ["Lodge-linked goods", "Design to lodge specifications; consistency and finishing; costing against imports; working capital for materials", "Design and quality coaching with lodge procurement teams; tooling and equipment finance; order-backed working capital", "Anchor operators, NIPDB, LEA, ZimTrade, ZDA, training institutions"],
      ["Crafts and cultural products", "Product differentiation; buyer relationships; pricing; export compliance", "Market-led design; buyer brokerage and curated retail; e-commerce listing; finance only after orders", "Craft hubs, BTO, NTB, ZimTrade, tourism associations"],
      ["Guiding and activities", "Guiding quality and certification; equipment; online visibility; seasonal cash flow", "Guide certification; lease-to-own equipment; digital marketing; seasonal or revenue-linked repayment", "Guides associations, training institutions, DMCs, youth funds"],
      ["Transport and transfers", "Tax clearance; insurance; vehicle maintenance; sub-hire contracts", "Compliance support through one-stop centres; asset finance with seasonal schedules; sub-hire contract templates", "ZIDA, CIPA, ZDA, banks' asset-finance units, DMCs"],
      ["Waste, recycling and cleaning", "Business model; service contracts; equipment; health and safety", "Service-contract brokerage with lodges and towns; equipment grants; youth incubation", "Local authorities, anchor lodges, NGOs, incubators"],
      ["Nature-positive products", "Supply volume; certification; governance; export readiness", "Certification grants; processing equipment; governance support; export order finance", "Conservancies, CBNRM NGOs, CEEC, ZDA, export promotion bodies"],
      ["Accommodation (community)", "Governance; JV negotiation; service standards; tenure", "Governance and JV advisory; grading and service training; blended finance", "BTO, NTB, Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, NACSO, CAMPFIRE Association"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Food and agricultural supply.** Producers need to aggregate, deliver consistent quality and volume, meet food-safety and packaging standards, obtain VAT registration and tax clearance, and bridge the cash-flow gap between delivery and payment. The recommended response is producer-group formation, irrigation and post-harvest support, supply agreements with lodges and contract-backed working capital, delivered by anchor lodges with LEA, SMEDCO, CEEC, agricultural extension services and conservancies." },
      { p: "**Lodge-linked goods.** Makers need to design to lodge specifications, improve consistency and finishing, cost their products against imports and finance materials ahead of delivery. Design and quality coaching with lodge procurement teams, tooling and equipment finance and order-backed working capital address these gaps, with anchor operators, NIPDB, LEA, ZimTrade, ZDA and training institutions as delivery partners." },
      { p: "**Crafts and cultural products.** The gaps are product differentiation, buyer relationships, pricing and export compliance. Market-led design, buyer brokerage and curated retail, and e-commerce listing should come first, with finance offered only once orders exist. Craft hubs, BTO, the Namibia Tourism Board, ZimTrade and tourism associations are the natural partners." },
      { p: "**Guiding and activities.** Guides need quality improvements and certification, equipment, online visibility and help managing seasonal cash flow. Guide certification, lease-to-own equipment, digital marketing support and seasonal or revenue-linked repayment respond to these needs, delivered with guides associations, training institutions, DMCs and youth funds." },
      { p: "**Transport and transfers.** Operators need tax clearance, insurance, vehicle maintenance skills and sub-hire contracts. Compliance support through one-stop centres, asset finance on seasonal schedules and standard sub-hire contract templates are recommended, working with ZIDA, CIPA, ZDA, banks' asset-finance units and DMCs." },
      { p: "**Waste, recycling and cleaning.** Enterprises need a viable business model, service contracts, equipment and health and safety practices. Brokering service contracts with lodges and towns, equipment grants and youth incubation are recommended, delivered with local authorities, anchor lodges, NGOs and incubators." },
      { p: "**Nature-positive products.** The gaps are supply volume, certification, governance and export readiness. Certification grants, processing equipment, governance support and finance against export orders are recommended, with conservancies, CBNRM NGOs, CEEC, ZDA and export promotion bodies as partners." },
      { p: "**Community accommodation.** Community enterprises need stronger governance, better JV negotiation, higher service standards and secure tenure. Governance and JV advisory support, grading and service training and blended finance are recommended, delivered with BTO, the Namibia Tourism Board, the Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, NACSO and the CAMPFIRE Association." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Business Support Organisations" },
  { p: "BSOs in KAZA include public agencies (INAPEM in Angola, LEA in Botswana, NIPDB in Namibia, ZDA and CEEC in Zambia, SMEDCO in Zimbabwe), associations (such as WIBA in Botswana and the tourism associations in each state), conservation NGOs and community institutions. Section 7.3 profiles the main organisations in each country. Their gaps are:" },
  { b: [
    "**Generic rather than sector-specific services.** Most BDS is general business training, not tourism supplier development or R-NBT product development.",
    "**Weak link to finance.** BSOs and financiers operate in silos, producing weak pipelines of investment-ready MSMEs (Financial Landscape Analysis). Few BSOs prepare loan applications or broker introductions to lenders. Where BSOs are also lenders (CEDA, CEEC, SMEDCO), mentoring tends to follow a loan rather than prepare an MSME for one.",
    "**Limited reach in KAZA hubs.** Services are concentrated in capitals; outreach to Kasane, Maun, Katima Mulilo, Rundu, Livingstone, Hwange and Cuando Cubango is thin. INAPEM's first decentralised incubation programme, for example, is in Huíla province rather than Cuando Cubango (Section 7.3).",
    "**Institutional uncertainty.** Some lead agencies are being restructured or are rebuilding systems. Botswana has long-standing plans to rationalise parastatals with overlapping mandates, which unions have questioned (Business Weekly, 2026), and Namibia's BIPA is still procuring a new online registration system (Section 7.1). This makes it harder for BSOs to commit to multi-year supplier-development programmes.",
    "**No diagnostic or tracking tools.** BSOs lack a common readiness diagnostic and do not track MSMEs' progress from tier to tier.",
    "**Gender and youth capacity.** Few BSOs have staff or methods for gender-transformative or youth-specific support.",
    "**Funding.** The 2025 wind-down of USAID programmes removed grant and technical-assistance funding for many community enterprise programmes (Financial Landscape Analysis).",
  ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Financial Institutions" },
  { b: [
    "**Seasonal cash-flow lending:** structuring repayment around tourism and agricultural seasons rather than monthly amortisation.",
    "**Sector knowledge:** understanding R-NBT business models, anchor-buyer contracts, concessions and JV arrangements.",
    "**Alternative collateral and data:** using purchase orders, receivables, movable-asset registries, mobile-money data and savings-group records in credit assessment. Zambia's 4th-place ranking for getting credit in Doing Business 2020 shows that the legal tools exist there; Zimbabwe launched a collateral registry for movable assets in 2022, and Botswana's CIPA is developing a National Collateral Registry for SME credit. The gap is lenders' use of these tools for small borrowers. In Angola, which ranked 185th for getting credit, lenders have few such tools and need contractual workarounds such as payment routing through the anchor buyer.",
    "**Small-ticket economics:** reducing the unit cost of small loans through digital origination, group or value-chain lending, and BSO pre-screening.",
    "**Guarantee use:** many banks under-use existing credit guarantee schemes; staff need to understand when and how to use them.",
    "**Gender-smart lending:** collecting sex-disaggregated portfolio data, designing products for women-owned enterprises and training staff to reduce bias.",
    "**Regenerative screening:** applying simple environmental and social criteria consistent with a KAZA R-NBT eligibility framework.",
  ] },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Summary of capacity gaps by actor and priority",
    cols: [1900, 3626, 1300, 2200],
    head: ["Actor", "Priority capacity gaps", "Priority", "Chains most affected"],
    rows: [
      ["MSMEs (producers, node A)", "Aggregation, quality, basic records, savings discipline", "High", "Food, crafts, nature-positive"],
      ["MSMEs (suppliers and services, nodes C-D)", "Reliability, compliance, contracting, cash-flow planning, investment readiness", "High", "Lodge-linked goods, food, transport, guiding"],
      ["Cooperatives and community enterprises (nodes B, E)", "Governance, benefit-sharing, certification, leadership including women and youth", "High", "Nature-positive, crafts, accommodation"],
      ["BSOs", "Sector-specific supplier development; readiness diagnostic; finance brokerage; KAZA outreach; gender and youth methods", "High", "All"],
      ["Financial institutions", "Seasonal structuring; alternative collateral; guarantee use; gender-disaggregated data", "Medium-High", "All, especially supply chains"],
      ["Anchor buyers", "Supplier-development commitment; payment terms that support MSME cash flow; contract templates", "Medium", "Food, lodge-linked goods, transport"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**In summary,** four groups have high-priority capacity gaps. Producers at node A need aggregation, better quality, basic records and savings discipline, above all in food, crafts and nature-positive products. Suppliers and service enterprises at nodes C and D need reliability, compliance, contracting skills, cash-flow planning and investment readiness, particularly in lodge-linked goods, food, transport and guiding. Cooperatives and community enterprises at nodes B and E need governance, benefit-sharing rules, certification and leadership that includes women and youth, especially in nature-positive products, crafts and accommodation. BSOs, across all chains, need sector-specific supplier development, a readiness diagnostic, finance brokerage, outreach in the KAZA hubs and gender and youth methods." },
      { p: "Financial institutions' gaps are of medium to high priority and affect all chains, especially supply chains: seasonal structuring, alternative collateral, guarantee use and gender-disaggregated data. Anchor buyers' gaps are of medium priority but matter for food, lodge-linked goods and transport: a commitment to supplier development, payment terms that support MSME cash flow, and standard contract templates." },
    ] },

  { h: 1, t: "Enabling Environment" },
  { p: "This chapter evaluates the conditions outside the enterprise that shape investment readiness: national and regional regulation, market access and links with larger tourism operators, business development services, and wider policy conditions. Section 7.1 draws on the World Bank's Doing Business 2020 country reports to compare the five states. Section 7.2 adds evidence on market access from sources outside the Macro Diagnostic, and Section 7.3 profiles the main BSOs in each country." },

  { h: 2, t: "National and Regional Regulatory Frameworks" },
  { h: 3, t: "What the Doing Business country reports show" },
  { p: "The World Bank's Doing Business 2020 economy profiles are the most recent source that measures business regulation in all five KAZA states in the same way. They score ten areas of regulation that a small or medium-sized domestic limited company meets over its life, from starting a business to resolving insolvency, and rank 190 economies. The data were collected to May 2019 and describe the largest business city in each country (Luanda, Gaborone, Windhoek, Lusaka and Harare), not the KAZA hubs, where procedures are usually slower and offices further away. The series was discontinued in 2021, so the rankings are best read as a comparison between the five states and a guide to which procedures weigh most on MSMEs." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Doing Business 2020: overall and topic rankings of the five KAZA states (out of 190 economies)",
    cols: [2626, 1280, 1280, 1280, 1280, 1280],
    head: ["Indicator", "Angola", "Botswana", "Namibia", "Zambia", "Zimbabwe"],
    rows: [
      ["**Ease of doing business: rank (score out of 100)**", "**177 (41.3)**", "**87 (66.2)**", "**104 (61.4)**", "**85 (66.9)**", "**140 (54.5)**"],
      ["Starting a business", "146", "159", "165", "117", "167"],
      ["Dealing with construction permits", "120", "44", "84", "67", "140"],
      ["Getting electricity", "156", "139", "76", "129", "167"],
      ["Registering property", "167", "82", "173", "149", "109"],
      ["Getting credit", "185", "80", "80", "4", "67"],
      ["Protecting minority investors", "147", "72", "88", "72", "97"],
      ["Paying taxes", "106", "59", "n.a. (score 74.5)", "17", "146"],
      ["Trading across borders", "174", "55", "138", "155", "159"],
      ["Enforcing contracts", "186", "137", "64", "130", "169"],
      ["Resolving insolvency", "168", "84", "127", "79", "142"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Overall rankings.** Of 190 economies, Zambia ranked 85th (score 66.9 out of 100) and Botswana 87th (66.2), the two easiest places to do business among the KAZA states. Namibia followed at 104th (61.4), Zimbabwe at 140th (54.5) and Angola at 177th (41.3)." },
      { p: "**Topic rankings by country.** **Angola** ranked 146th for starting a business, 120th for construction permits, 156th for getting electricity, 167th for registering property, 185th for getting credit, 147th for protecting minority investors, 106th for paying taxes, 174th for trading across borders, 186th for enforcing contracts and 168th for resolving insolvency. **Botswana** ranked 159th for starting a business, 44th for construction permits, 139th for electricity, 82nd for property, 80th for credit, 72nd for minority investors, 59th for taxes, 55th for trading across borders, 137th for enforcing contracts and 84th for insolvency. **Namibia** ranked 165th for starting a business, 84th for construction permits, 76th for electricity, 173rd for property, 80th for credit, 88th for minority investors, 138th for trading across borders, 64th for enforcing contracts and 127th for insolvency; its paying-taxes score was 74.5, but its rank could not be confirmed. **Zambia** ranked 117th for starting a business, 67th for construction permits, 129th for electricity, 149th for property, 4th for credit, 72nd for minority investors, 17th for taxes, 155th for trading across borders, 130th for enforcing contracts and 79th for insolvency. **Zimbabwe** ranked 167th for starting a business, 140th for construction permits, 167th for electricity, 109th for property, 67th for credit, 97th for minority investors, 146th for taxes, 159th for trading across borders, 169th for enforcing contracts and 142nd for insolvency." },
    ] },
  { p: "Source: World Bank, Doing Business 2020 report and economy profiles. Topic ranks were transcribed from the published profiles; the overall ranks and scores are confirmed in the World Bank's published rankings." },
  { p: "Five findings stand out for R-NBT MSMEs:" },
  { b: [
    "**Formalising is slow everywhere.** All five states ranked 117th or lower for starting a business. Registering a company took about 8.5 days in Zambia, 27 days and 9 procedures in Zimbabwe, 36 days and 8 procedures in Angola, and 54 days and 10 procedures in Namibia. In Botswana in 2020, company registration could be completed online in a day, but tax registration took about 30 days; later US government reports put tax registration at about a week (US Department of State, 2020 and 2026). For a micro-enterprise earning most of its income in a five-month season, a registration process that takes one to two months has a real opportunity cost. This supports treating formalisation as a milestone tied to a contract (Section 4.3) and offering hands-on help with procedures.",
    "**Credit infrastructure varies widely.** Zambia ranked 4th in the world for getting credit (score 95 of 100), reflecting strong legal rights for borrowers and lenders over movable assets and good credit-information coverage. Zimbabwe (67th), Botswana and Namibia (both 80th) were mid-ranked, while Angola ranked 185th (score 5 of 100). Where the legal framework is strong, receivables and movable-asset lending to suppliers is legally feasible and the constraint is lender practice; in Angola, finance will depend for some time on grants, guarantees and contractual arrangements with anchor buyers.",
    "**Contracts are hard to enforce.** Enforcing a commercial contract through the courts took about 660 days in Botswana (137th) and 410 days at a cost of 83% of the claim in Zimbabwe (169th); Angola ranked 186th and Zambia 130th. Only Namibia (64th) ranked in the top half. Contract-backed supplier finance in KAZA therefore cannot rely on court enforcement. It needs arrangements in which the anchor buyer pays the lender directly, as in the Wilderness-Stanbic facility, where suppliers cede their right to receive payment from Wilderness to the bank (Mmegi, 2025).",
    "**Tax compliance burdens differ.** Zambia ranked 17th for paying taxes and Botswana 59th, but Zimbabwe ranked 146th, and Namibia's tax system required 27 payments and 302 hours a year for the standard firm. Combined with the 30% withholding tax on suppliers without tax clearance in Zimbabwe (MD p. 74), this explains why tax clearance is the formalisation step that matters most to anchor buyers.",
    "**Cross-border trade is costly.** Four of the five states ranked 138th or lower for trading across borders; only Botswana (55th) performed well. This matters in a transfrontier area where Hwange lodges, Kasane suppliers and Livingstone hotels could source across borders. Small traders face border procedures designed for larger consignments (see the regional frameworks below).",
  ] },
  { p: "Two further indicators affect specific chains. Getting electricity was slow and costly in Zimbabwe (167th), Angola (156th), Botswana (139th) and Zambia (129th), which raises the cost of processing, cold storage and workshop production for food, nature-positive products and lodge-linked goods, and strengthens the case for solar equipment finance. Registering property was difficult in Namibia (173rd), Angola (167th) and Zambia (149th); although most R-NBT MSMEs operate on communal land that cannot be mortgaged in any case, the rankings show that even freehold property is a slow route to collateral." },
  { h: 3, t: "Country profiles" },
  { p: "**Angola** (177th) had the weakest business environment of the five, with bottom-ten rankings for getting credit and enforcing contracts. Government has tried to simplify entry: Presidential Decree 40/12 created the Balcão Único do Empreendedor (BUE) in 2012 to simplify the formation and licensing of micro and small enterprises and bring informal activity into the formal economy, and by 2021 a new platform at the Guiché Único da Empresa allowed a company to be created online or in person in about an hour when documents were in order (Ver Angola, 2021). INAPEM certification gives access to tax benefits, and banks ask for the certificate when a company applies for finance (AllAfrica, 2026). Sector rules cap foreign shareholding at 49% in road passenger transport and 50% in road freight (MD p. 26). For Cuando Cubango, the binding constraints are a thin enterprise base, distance from Luanda-based services, and the fact that guiding and logistics for Angolan sites accrue in Namibia (MD p. 118-119)." },
  { p: "**Botswana** (87th) combined good rankings for construction permits (44th), trading across borders (55th) and paying taxes (59th) with weak rankings for starting a business (159th), getting electricity (139th) and enforcing contracts (137th). The Companies and Intellectual Property Authority (CIPA) runs an online business registration system, which the Ministry of Trade and Entrepreneurship credits with faster turnaround times. Its 2026/27 budget speech also reported that the World Bank's B-READY 2025 report recognised Botswana's strong legal frameworks and CIPA's digital front door, but flagged insolvency, tax compliance, dispute resolution and market competition as areas needing attention; it announced a home-grown company registration system and a National Collateral Registry to improve SME access to credit (Ministry of Trade and Entrepreneurship, 2026). Sector rules are the most protective of citizen MSMEs in the region: fresh-produce and general-dealer licences, guesthouses and touristic transfer companies are reserved for citizens (MD p. 31-34), Botswana law reserves 35 service sectors for citizens (US International Trade Administration), and the Economic Inclusion Act 2021, in force since April 2022, provides for the participation of targeted citizens in the economy, including through procurement and licensing. These rules create protected demand for citizen suppliers but close processing, distribution and transport to outside equity." },
  { p: "**Namibia** (104th) ranked well for getting electricity (76th) and enforcing contracts (64th), the best of the five states, but poorly for starting a business (165th) and registering property (173rd). Formalisation remains the weak point. In June 2024 the Business and Intellectual Property Authority (BIPA) said it was procuring an integrated online registration system expected within 18 to 24 months (The Brief, 2024); in May 2025 the responsible minister said the existing online system was not working (The Namibian, 2025); and in August 2025 BIPA announced that it would de-register about 1,000 non-compliant companies a month (The Brief, 2025). Namibia was among the new economies covered by the B-READY 2025 report (Ecofin, 2026). In tourism, Namibia has a mature conservancy JV framework but low MSME value capture (MD p. 37, 40), and the end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025 has affected demand from Europe (Section 7.2)." },
  { p: "**Zambia** (85th) had the best overall ranking and strong scores for getting credit (4th) and paying taxes (17th), but weak ones for trading across borders (155th), registering property (149th) and enforcing contracts (130th). Licensing is the main recurring burden: more than a quarter of Zambian firms cite business licensing and permits as a major obstacle, nearly double the regional average, and Zambia is moving to risk-based regulation with World Bank support (World Bank, 2024). After a 2025 benchmarking mission to Mauritius, the Business Regulatory Review Agency (BRRA) began talks with the Ministry of Tourism and the Zambia Tourism Agency on adopting omnibus licensing, in which one licence covers several activities, for the accommodation sub-sector (Zambia Monitor, 2025). Road service licences require at least 25% Zambian membership and all-Zambian directors unless the applicant holds a ZDA licence (MD p. 44), which favours local transport MSMEs." },
  { p: "**Zimbabwe** (140th) rose 15 places in Doing Business 2020 and was among the World Bank's top 20 improvers, with reforms in five areas; improvements in starting a business, including lower business licensing fees in Harare, were the main driver (World Bank, 2019; The Herald). It still ranked low for starting a business (167th), getting electricity (167th), paying taxes (146th) and enforcing contracts (169th). The Zimbabwe Investment and Development Agency (ZIDA) runs a One Stop Investment Services Centre, and in January 2024 ZIDA and ZIMRA signed a memorandum of understanding to integrate their electronic and investor services (ZIMRA, 2024). Ownership rules have tightened. In 2018 the indigenisation law was narrowed to a list of sectors reserved for citizens that included passenger transport and the provision, marketing and distribution of local arts and crafts (Veritas, 2018). Statutory Instrument 215 of 2025 then set new rules for foreign participation in reserved sectors; press reports describe 14 reserved sectors and a requirement for foreign-owned businesses in them to transfer a 75% controlling stake to citizens within three years, although reports differ on the details (ZimLII; Daily News, 2025; Business Times, 2026). This protects MSME space in transport and crafts but deters foreign joint-venture capital in those chains." },
  { h: 3, t: "Sector-specific rules in the priority chains" },
  { p: "Regulation also shapes which MSMEs can exist in each chain and who can invest in them. The Macro Diagnostic's findings and the later rules described above combine as follows." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Sector-specific regulatory factors affecting MSME readiness in the priority chains",
    cols: [1300, 4300, 3426],
    head: ["Country", "Factor", "Effect on readiness and investment"],
    rows: [
      ["Botswana", "Fresh-produce and general-dealer retail licences reserved for citizens; small-scale food and beverage manufacturing closed to foreign shareholding (MD p. 31). Foreign equity banned in touristic transfer companies (MD p. 32, 34). Guesthouses citizen-reserved (MD p. 34). Economic Inclusion Act 2021 promotes participation of targeted citizens", "Protects MSME space and creates demand for citizen suppliers, but closes processing, distribution and transport to outside capital; growth depends on domestic finance and lodge or DMC contracting"],
      ["Zambia", "Road service licences require at least 25% Zambian membership and all-Zambian directors unless the applicant holds a ZDA licence (MD p. 44). Licensing burden high; omnibus licensing for accommodation under discussion", "Favours local transport MSMEs; limits joint ventures with foreign partners; licensing cost falls on micro-enterprises"],
      ["Angola", "Foreign shareholding capped at 49% in road passenger transport and 50% in road freight (MD p. 26). INAPEM certification requested by banks for loans", "Requires local partners; thin local enterprise base in Cuando Cubango; certification is an extra step before finance"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "Withholding tax of 30% on payments to suppliers without tax clearance (MD p. 74; operator-reported). Reserved sectors for citizens include passenger transport and local arts and crafts; SI 215 of 2025 tightened rules on foreign participation", "Strong penalty on informality; makes tax clearance a precondition for supplying anchors; protects MSME space in transport and crafts but deters foreign JV capital"],
      ["Namibia", "Conservancy JV framework; end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025, with demand shifting (MD p. 117-118); slow company registration", "Mature community framework but low MSME value capture; demand shifts affect supplier viability; registration delays slow formalisation"],
      ["Regional", "KAZA Univisa operates for Zambia and Zimbabwe only; COMESA Simplified Trade Regime covers Zambia and Zimbabwe only; cross-border supply chains less evidenced than visitor flows (MD p. 117-118)", "Limits cross-border sourcing and multi-country itineraries for MSMEs"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**In Botswana**, fresh-produce and general-dealer retail licences are reserved for citizens, small-scale food and beverage manufacturing is closed to foreign shareholding (MD p. 31), foreign equity is banned in touristic transfer companies (MD p. 32, 34) and guesthouses are reserved for citizens (MD p. 34), while the Economic Inclusion Act 2021 promotes the participation of targeted citizens. Together these rules protect MSME space and create demand for citizen suppliers, but they close processing, distribution and transport to outside capital, so growth depends on domestic finance and lodge or DMC contracts." },
      { p: "**In Zambia**, road service licences require at least 25% Zambian membership and all-Zambian directors unless the applicant holds a ZDA licence (MD p. 44). This favours local transport MSMEs but limits joint ventures with foreign partners, and the general licensing burden falls heavily on micro-enterprises, which is why omnibus licensing for accommodation is under discussion. **In Angola**, foreign shareholding is capped at 49% in road passenger transport and 50% in road freight (MD p. 26), which requires local partners in a province where the local enterprise base is thin, and the INAPEM certificate that banks request adds a step before finance." },
      { p: "**In Zimbabwe**, an anchor operator reports a 30% withholding tax on payments to suppliers without tax clearance (MD p. 74), a strong penalty on informality that makes tax clearance a precondition for supplying anchors. Passenger transport and local arts and crafts are reserved for citizens, and SI 215 of 2025 tightened the rules on foreign participation, protecting MSME space in these chains while deterring foreign JV capital. **In Namibia**, the conservancy JV framework is mature but MSME value capture is low, the end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025 has shifted some demand (MD p. 117-118), which affects supplier viability, and slow company registration delays formalisation. **At regional level**, the KAZA Univisa and the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime both cover only Zambia and Zimbabwe, and cross-border supply chains are less evidenced than visitor flows (MD p. 117-118), which limits cross-border sourcing and multi-country itineraries for MSMEs." },
    ] },
  { h: 3, t: "Regional frameworks" },
  { p: "The KAZA Treaty and the SADC Protocol on Trade provide the framework for transfrontier tourism and trade, but MSMEs feel the regional frameworks mainly through visas and border procedures. The **KAZA Univisa** is used in Zambia and Zimbabwe and covers day trips into Botswana through Kazungula. At the KAZA heads of state summit in Livingstone in 2024, the five Partner States agreed in principle to broaden its use, and Botswana's Vice President said Botswana would fully adopt it, but no implementation date was given (TimesLIVE, 2024). Multi-country itineraries, which favour guiding, transfer and activity MSMEs along the Victoria Falls-Chobe corridor, therefore remain more costly to sell than they need to be." },
  { p: "For small-scale suppliers, the **COMESA Simplified Trade Regime** (STR) allows traders to move small consignments across borders with simplified documents. The COMESA threshold is USD 2,000 per consignment, adopted in 2014 and recommended to be maintained in a 2025 review (COMESA, 2025), but Zimbabwe has applied a lower limit because of exchange-control rules (ZIMRA). The STR applies between Zambia and Zimbabwe, both COMESA members, but not to Botswana, Namibia or Angola. Uptake has been low. A simplified regime that also covered the other KAZA states, together with awareness and border-staff practice at the Kazungula one-stop border post, could help women traders and small producers supply lodges across borders." },
  { p: "**Implications for readiness.** Regulation in KAZA is not the main reason MSMEs are not investment-ready, but it raises the cost of every step on the readiness pathway: registration takes weeks, tax clearance is costly in Zimbabwe, contracts cannot be relied on in court, and cross-border sourcing is hard. Programmes should therefore (i) help MSMEs through registration and tax clearance when a contract requires it, using one-stop centres where they work; (ii) design supplier finance around anchor-buyer payment routing rather than court enforcement; (iii) use Zambia's movable-collateral framework, Zimbabwe's collateral registry and Botswana's planned National Collateral Registry for small-supplier lending; and (iv) work with the KAZA Secretariat on Univisa adoption and a simplified regime for small cross-border suppliers." },

  { h: 2, t: "Market Access and Linkages with Larger Operators" },
  { p: "The Macro Diagnostic's findings on market access are clear. Demand exists: lodges want to buy locally, and the constraint is supplier reliability and readiness (MD p. 70-71, 128, 132). Entry can be quick once a supplier is ready; in Zimbabwe, new suppliers typically need only two or three successful bookings before being listed regularly (MD p. 75). Buyers set the bar: reliability matters more than price, new suppliers must match the largest incumbent supplier on price and quality, and support is best directed at enterprises already showing momentum (MD p. 68). Marketing, operator confidence and trade-show costs are obstacles for new suppliers (MD p. 75), and limited airline competition and high fares constrain the Victoria Falls corridor (MD p. 84, 117). Evidence from outside the diagnostic supports these findings and adds detail on the size of the market, what anchor procurement programmes have achieved, and what happens when linkages are weak." },
  { h: 3, t: "The size and direction of tourism demand" },
  { p: "Visitor demand has largely recovered from the pandemic, but it is volatile and policy-sensitive. Namibia received 1,345,168 international visitors in 2025, 6.9% fewer than the 1,444,174 recorded in 2024, while tourist arrivals alone fell by 3.2% (The Namibian, 2026). European tourist arrivals fell by 21%, a drop the tourism ministry linked both to the April 2025 change in visa policy and to safety concerns (The Namibian, 2026). Visitors from non-African countries now pay N$1,600 for a visa on arrival, which the tourism federation FENATA describes as one of the highest fees in the region, and operators reported delays and inconsistent implementation in the first months (Tourism Update, 2025). In Zimbabwe, international arrivals rose by 27% to 751,483 in the first half of 2024, and tourism receipts, including domestic spending, reached an estimated USD 493 million in that half-year (Equity Axis, 2025, citing UN Tourism). Botswana recorded between 391,264 and 421,744 international tourist arrivals a quarter in the second to fourth quarters of 2024, about three-quarters of them from SADC countries (Statistics Botswana, 2026). For MSMEs, the lesson is that demand is large enough to support local supply chains but can shift between neighbouring destinations quickly, as the Macro Diagnostic observes for Namibia and Kasane/Chobe (MD p. 117-118). Suppliers with several buyers, or buyers in more than one destination, are more resilient." },
  { h: 3, t: "What anchor procurement programmes show" },
  { p: "**Wilderness in Botswana** is the best-documented case of anchor-led local procurement in KAZA. It reports buying about BWP 292 million a year from Botswana-registered businesses, more than 80% of its procurement by value (Daily News, 2025), and its 2026 impact report puts the share at more than 86% when aviation, which must source parts internationally, is excluded (ATTA, 2026). Local procurement is not the same as MSME procurement, however. Wilderness's first Local Supplier Expo in Maun in 2024 hosted 110 local vendors and led to more than BWP 6.2 million of new contracts for citizen-owned businesses, a figure that had passed BWP 8 million by mid-2025 (Daily News, 2025), and 68 businesses have joined its supplier development programme (Mmegi, 2026). New contracts of this size are equivalent to about 3% of the company's annual local purchases, which shows both that small citizen suppliers can be brought into a large operator's supply chain and how much room remains. The programme is linked to finance through a 2025 memorandum with Stanbic Bank Botswana, under which Stanbic lends to SMMEs holding Wilderness supply contracts without traditional collateral and is repaid from contract payments that the suppliers cede to the bank (Mmegi, 2025), and to BDS through a 2026 memorandum with LEA that launched a Citizen Economic Empowerment Programme (Mmegi, 2026)." },
  { p: "**Gondwana Collection in Namibia** supports Namibian-owned suppliers and wholesalers and diversifies its supplier base by supporting small enterprises near its lodges (Gondwana Collection). At its Etosha King Nehale lodge, more than 80% of the items in the curio shop are locally crafted (Gondwana Collection). This is a useful counterpoint to the finding that about 80% of lodge décor in the Kavango and Zambezi regions is imported (MD p. 70): where an operator builds local sourcing into design and procurement from the start, local suppliers can meet lodge specifications." },
  { p: "**Hotels in Livingstone** have brought smallholder farmers into their supply chains. The Royal Livingstone hotel runs a fortnightly smallholder farmers' market at which it buys crops directly from local smallholders, who previously relied on roadside sales and middlemen, and it helped save the Maramba Women's Mushroom Farm, whose members now sell most of their produce to local hotels and resorts under long-term contracts (Anantara, operator-reported). A University of Pretoria doctoral study of tourism-agriculture linkages in Livingstone found that local procurement is the dominant channel and includes smallholders, but that supply, demand and marketing constraints limit how deeply smallholders are integrated (Nsanzya, University of Pretoria). These findings mirror the Macro Diagnostic's evidence from Hwange (MD p. 75)." },
  { h: 3, t: "The cost of weak linkages" },
  { p: "Earlier research shows what is at stake. A study of the Okavango Delta found that foreign ownership of tourism facilities led to revenue being repatriated, that expatriates dominated management, and that weak links with the domestic economy, particularly agriculture, limited tourism's contribution to rural development (Mbaiwa, 2005). The problem persists: Botswana's tourism ministry has said it is reviewing the Tourism Act to require tourists to pay in full in Botswana, in local currency, because of revenue repatriation, and is finalising a Tourism Leakages Study (Daily News). ODI's value-chain studies of tourism destinations found that the share of destination revenue reaching poor households ranged from about 5% to more than 25% depending on context (Ashley and Mitchell, 2007). The priority chains identified in this assessment are the main levers for raising that share in KAZA." },
  { h: 3, t: "Implications for linkage programmes" },
  { b: [
    "**Work through procurement teams, not only enterprises.** The Wilderness and Gondwana cases show that local sourcing rises when anchors set targets, specify products in advance and nominate suppliers for development.",
    "**Use anchor programmes as the gateway to finance.** Contract-backed finance (Wilderness-Stanbic) and supplier-development BDS (Wilderness-LEA) can be replicated with other operators and banks in Botswana, Zambia and Zimbabwe.",
    "**Track MSME share as well as local share.** Destination baselines (MD p. 140-141) should record how much of local procurement goes to MSMEs, women- and youth-led enterprises and community enterprises, not only to local companies.",
    "**Build multi-buyer and cross-border supply.** Aggregators that can supply several lodges, or lodges on both sides of a border, reduce the risk that demand shifts between destinations.",
    "**Address payment terms.** Micro-suppliers often finance goods for weeks between delivery and payment; faster payment by the anchor, or receivables finance, should be part of any supplier-development agreement.",
  ] },

  { h: 2, t: "Business Development Services" },
  { p: "BDS is available in all five states but is generic, concentrated in capitals and weakly connected to finance (Section 6.2). The Macro Diagnostic recommends linking supplier development to real contracts: where a supplier has a credible buyer but lacks capacity, certification, systems or working capital, the programme should close that specific constraint (MD p. 147). This section profiles the main BSOs in each country, their current programmes and how far they reach into KAZA." },
  { h: 3, t: "Angola" },
  { p: "**INAPEM** (Instituto Nacional de Apoio às Micro, Pequenas e Médias Empresas) is the lead public BSO. It certifies MSMEs, which gives access to tax benefits and is requested by banks for loan applications; in Benguela province alone it certified 3,595 enterprises in 2025, of which 3,137 were micro, 321 small and 137 medium (AllAfrica, 2026). It also provides training and business-plan support. The EU-funded Envolver project (2019-2024) qualified 2,384 MSMEs and trained 574 financial-institution staff and 376 mentors, and its successor, Envolver+, an EU twinning project led by Portugal's IAPMEI with Spain and Germany, is helping INAPEM to create and manage a national network of certified incubators between September 2025 and June 2028 with a budget of EUR 2.5 million (Ver Angola, 2025; FIIAPP). INAPEM's first decentralised incubation programme, Tuendi, started in Huíla province (Forbes África Lusófona, 2025). Other BSOs include the one-stop **Balcão Único do Empreendedor** and Guiché Único da Empresa for registration, **AIPEX** for investment facilitation, the **Câmara de Comércio e Indústria de Angola**, and Luanda-based hospitality and travel associations. In Cuando Cubango, the provincial government, **MINTUR**, the conservation area authority and NGOs such as The HALO Trust and the National Geographic Okavango Wilderness Project are more present than enterprise BSOs (Financial Landscape Analysis). No tourism-specific BDS was identified in the KAZA area." },
  { h: 3, t: "Botswana" },
  { p: "The **Local Enterprise Authority (LEA)** is the lead BSO for citizen MSMEs, providing business development, mentoring and supplier-readiness support, including record-keeping support to tourism enterprises (Financial Landscape Analysis). Its 2026 memorandum of understanding with Wilderness, which launched a Citizen Economic Empowerment Programme to bring more citizen-owned businesses into the company's supply chain, is the most direct tourism supplier-development partnership in KAZA (Mmegi, 2026). **CEDA** provides citizen enterprise finance with post-loan mentoring. The **Botswana Tourism Organisation (BTO)** supports grading, product development and citizen tourism enterprise. **HATAB** represents the hospitality industry and has a partnership with Stanbic Bank Botswana to advance tourism, **WIBA** supports women entrepreneurs, and the **Botswana Guides Association** and **BOCOBONET** represent guides and community trusts (Financial Landscape Analysis). Private anchor programmes, notably Wilderness's supplier development programme and Local Supplier Expo, now act as BSOs in their own right (Section 7.2)." },
  { h: 3, t: "Namibia" },
  { p: "The **Namibia Investment Promotion and Development Board (NIPDB)** is the national coordinator of MSME development across sectors (NIPDB). Its K2G High Potential Pool supports export-ready micro and small businesses with at least 51% Namibian ownership for 36 months; NIPDB does not lend or give grants itself, but partner banks pledged N$300,000 to the pool in 2023 (NIPDB). With the National Planning Commission and GIZ, NIPDB also runs an N$18 million SME fund financed by the German development ministry, which offers grants of N$50,000 to N$100,000 with pitch coaching and business advice and gives priority to youth-owned (aged 18 to 34) and women-owned businesses, social enterprises and cooperatives (The Namibian). **BIPA** handles registration (Section 7.1). The **Namibia Chamber of Commerce and Industry** has branches in the Zambezi and Kavango regions, and the **Namibia Tourism Board**, **FENATA**, **HAN** and the Tour and Safari Association represent and register tourism businesses. For community enterprise, **NACSO**, **IRDNC** and the **Namibia Nature Foundation** have long supported conservancies in the Zambezi and Kavango regions, and the **Development Bank of Namibia** combines tourism finance with advisory support (Financial Landscape Analysis). NIPDB's programmes are national and not tourism-specific, so a KAZA supplier-development service would need to be built with the conservancy support organisations and lodge operators." },
  { h: 3, t: "Zambia" },
  { p: "The **Zambia Development Agency (ZDA)** promotes MSME development and investment, and the **Citizens Economic Empowerment Commission (CEEC)** provides concessional finance. The two have signed an agreement to provide SMEs with both financial and non-financial business development services, enrol high-potential businesses in entrepreneurship programmes and link funded SMEs and cooperatives to supermarkets and regional markets such as COMESA (Zambia Monitor). The **Ministry of Small and Medium Enterprise Development** sets MSME policy, the **BRRA** leads licensing reform, and the **Zambia Chamber of Small and Medium Business Associations** represents MSMEs. In tourism, the **Tourism Council of Zambia** and the **Livingstone Tourism Association** represent operators, and the **Hotel and Tourism Training Institute** and Livingstone's tourism university provide skills (Financial Landscape Analysis). Youth-focused BDS is emerging: in June 2025, UNDP and the Ministry of Youth, Sport and Arts launched a programme, implemented by BongoHive, to support 200 youth-led MSMEs in Livingstone, Kitwe and Lusaka, with grants for the five best performers in a regional pitch competition (UNDP Zambia, 2025). **Peace Parks Foundation** supports community enterprise around Simalaha and Sioma Ngwezi." },
  { h: 3, t: "Zimbabwe" },
  { p: "The **Small and Medium Enterprises Development Corporation (SMEDCO)**, a state development finance institution, combines lending with capacity building for MSMEs and cooperatives. It is rolling out a USD 15 million programme to build commercial and industrial facilities for small businesses in key economic centres (Business Times), and it has signed a memorandum of understanding with the Zimbabwe Stock Exchange to help SMEs prepare for the new Zimbabwe Entrepreneurship Exchange (ZEEX), including support for formalisation, governance and investment readiness (Newsday, 2026). **ZIDA**'s one-stop centre handles investor registration, tax and licensing services (Section 7.1). **ZimTrade** supports market linkages and exports, and the **Zimbabwe Tourism Authority** registers and grades tourism businesses. The **Tourism Business Council of Zimbabwe**, the Hospitality Association of Zimbabwe and guiding and safari associations represent operators, and the **CAMPFIRE Association** represents communities in CAMPFIRE districts such as Hwange and Binga. Women- and youth-focused finance comes from the Zimbabwe Women's Microfinance Bank and EmpowerBank (Financial Landscape Analysis)." },
  { h: 3, t: "Regional and cross-cutting BSOs" },
  { p: "The **KAZA Secretariat** in Kasane coordinates transfrontier tourism, including the Univisa. Conservation NGOs (Peace Parks Foundation, WWF, Conservation International, the African Wildlife Foundation and national partners) support community enterprise, and training institutions in Maun, Katima Mulilo, Livingstone and Hwange could host incubation. Tourism associations exist in every state but rarely provide supplier development." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Main BSOs by country: services, KAZA reach and position on the readiness pathway",
    cols: [1100, 2500, 2300, 1300, 1826],
    head: ["Country", "Main BSOs", "Main services", "Presence in KAZA hubs", "Tiers served"],
    rows: [
      ["Angola", "INAPEM; BUE; AIPEX; CCIA; MINTUR", "Certification, registration, training, incubator network (under development)", "Low (no tourism BDS identified)", "0-1"],
      ["Botswana", "LEA; CEDA; BTO; HATAB; WIBA; Wilderness supplier programme", "BDS, mentoring, supplier readiness, citizen finance, product development", "Medium (Maun, Kasane)", "1-2"],
      ["Namibia", "NIPDB; BIPA; NCCI; NTB; FENATA/HAN; NACSO, IRDNC, NNF; DBN", "MSME coordination, High Potential Pool, SME fund, conservancy enterprise support", "Medium for conservancies; low for MSME BDS", "0-2"],
      ["Zambia", "ZDA; CEEC; BRRA; ZCSMBA; TCZ; Livingstone Tourism Association; UNDP youth programme", "BDS with finance linkage, licensing reform, youth MSME training", "Medium (Livingstone)", "1-2"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "SMEDCO; ZIDA; ZimTrade; ZTA; TBCZ; CAMPFIRE Association", "Lending and capacity building, MSME infrastructure, one-stop registration, export linkages", "Low-Medium (Victoria Falls, Hwange)", "1-2"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Comparing the five countries,** Angola's main BSOs (INAPEM, the BUE, AIPEX, the chamber of commerce and MINTUR) provide certification, registration, training and an incubator network still under development; their presence in the KAZA area is low and they mainly serve Tier 0 and Tier 1 enterprises. Botswana's BSOs (LEA, CEDA, BTO, HATAB, WIBA and the Wilderness supplier programme) offer BDS, mentoring, supplier readiness, citizen finance and product development, have a medium presence in Maun and Kasane, and serve Tiers 1 and 2. Namibia's BSOs (NIPDB, BIPA, the chamber of commerce, the Namibia Tourism Board, FENATA and HAN, the conservancy support organisations and DBN) cover MSME coordination, the High Potential Pool, the SME fund and conservancy enterprise support; their presence is medium for conservancies but low for MSME BDS, and they serve Tiers 0 to 2." },
      { p: "Zambia's BSOs (ZDA, CEEC, the BRRA, the SME chamber, the Tourism Council, the Livingstone Tourism Association and the UNDP youth programme) provide BDS linked to finance, licensing reform and youth MSME training, have a medium presence in Livingstone and serve Tiers 1 and 2. Zimbabwe's BSOs (SMEDCO, ZIDA, ZimTrade, the Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, the Tourism Business Council and the CAMPFIRE Association) offer lending and capacity building, MSME infrastructure, one-stop registration and export linkages; their presence in Victoria Falls and Hwange is low to medium and they serve Tiers 1 and 2." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Assessment.** The main BSOs serve Tiers 0 to 2 with generic services. None offers a tourism supplier-development service at scale in the KAZA hubs, and only the Wilderness-LEA partnership in Botswana links BDS to a specific anchor's procurement. The most promising entry points are programmes that already combine BDS with finance or market access: the ZDA-CEEC agreement, SMEDCO's programmes and its partnership with the stock exchange, NIPDB's High Potential Pool and SME fund, INAPEM's incubator network and the anchor supplier programmes. Strengthening these, rather than creating new BSOs, is the approach recommended in Section 10.5." },

  { h: 2, t: "Policy Conditions" },
  { b: [
    "Citizen-empowerment policies in Botswana and Zambia provide concessional finance (CEDA, YDF, CEEC) that suits Tier 1 and 2 MSMEs if adapted to seasonality. In Botswana, the diamond-revenue slump has constrained public financiers' liquidity (Financial Landscape Analysis).",
    "Credit guarantee schemes operate in Namibia, Zambia, Zimbabwe and Angola; Botswana has no operational national scheme (Financial Landscape Analysis).",
    "Currency instability in Zimbabwe, Zambia and Angola complicates lending to MSMEs whose revenue may be in foreign currency while costs are local, or the reverse.",
    "Local-ownership policies are tightening in Zimbabwe (SI 215 of 2025) and remain strong in Botswana. They protect MSME space but require finance to come from domestic sources or from structures that respect ownership limits.",
    "The Macro Diagnostic recommends that pilot destinations establish baselines on local procurement, supplier contract value and locally sourced products (MD p. 140-141).",
  ] },
];
